00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.950 align:start position:0%
 
this is julia manchester from the hill  in washington julia thanks for joining  us today  we're here to talk about the emerson  poll the a news now emerson college the  hill poll  that looks at voting  trends coming up in the uh november  election welcome  thank you for having me  julie let's talk right away about how  biden is doing uh there there are some  poll questions that talk about how he  would perform in a 2024 matchup with  donald trump and with  ron desantis  can you talk a little bit about  those poll questions and tell us about  biden's chances of winning in 2024  well in terms of winning in 2024 i think  the mood is rather bleak for biden um  look i think he's dealing with obviously  inflation we've hit 9.1 percent  inflation today in the us and i think  that's very much showing in this poll  and you also have a lot of democrats in  general who are unhappy with his job  performance as well as um you know other  voters so i think there's a number of  factors the national mood being one of  them that's really dragging  president biden down in this poll and in  other polls  there's a buzz this week in political  conversation after a new york times poll  came out on monday  that poll indicates that 64 percent of  democrats would prefer a new candidate  in 2024  is it realistic to think democrats might  go with another candidate  well there is certainly a lot of buzz  and we've actually seen one progressive  group roots action who's actually  calling on president biden not to run in  2024 seemingly suggesting that they  would want a progressive candidate to  run instead however right now president  biden is saying democrats want him to  run and he and the administration are  adamant that he will run at this point  however we are seeing chatter that we  rarely see with other former that we've  really seen rarely seen with other  former presidents about how you know  what's his political future even though  we're not even to his first midterm  elections so we're really and i know  this is a cliche we are in unprecedented  times there have been a number of  strange things that have happened over  the past six or seven years in u.s  politics so anything could happen at  this point but i would say biden is not  in a great place when it comes to his  approval ratings in the party and  outside the party  there's no recent precedent is there for  a sitting president to be  replaced by another candidate midterm  excuse me after a first term  not that i'm aware of no recent  precedent i mean we saw that president  trump still ran in that election  president obama before him so we really  do see the leaders of these parties  really galvanize their bases galvanize  the party apparatus and that party  apparatus gets behind them and the party  apparatus as of now when we look to the  establishment organizations within the  democratic party whether that's the dnc  the dscc  um the d triple c they're behind  president biden at least publicly i  think what we're seeing is these more  progressive groups within the democratic  party really starting to push back  against the president we've seen that  the progressive movement while they  haven't had that many uh major electoral  victories they are gaining prominence  progressive movement is spreading in the  democratic party to a certain degree so  their voices are getting louder and  they're drawing attention to how they  feel about president biden  back to the emerson poll uh 57 percent  of respondents in in our poll of 2000  people said they disapprove of the job  that biden is doing and 32.5 approve  uh when we dive into that a little bit  and look a little bit closer it shows  that 31 of the people polled who voted  for biden in the in the election now  disapprove of his performance and among  democrats 65 percent approve of the job  biden is doing  lower than the 70 percent found in the  new york times poll where does this  leave democrats with less than four  months until the midterm election  well it doesn't leave them in a good  place and i mean that's very jarring i  would say that would be a jarring sign  for democrats coming in a major swing  state like like nevada  democrats need to win nevada whether  it's at the gubernatorial level the  senate level and the house level there  are a number of major races toss-up  races happening in the state so if the  leader of the democratic party right now  is not looked favorably upon by  democrats i think that could lead to  some serious questions uh as to how big  will uh democratic turnout be in nevada  come november you know will uh  independent voters will democrats even  you know vote for the democratic party  or will they switch the republican party  i think we can definitely say that the  majority of democrats will likely vote  along party lines in the midterms but  what i mean is that we should really  look at those independent or swing  voters in areas like las vegas for  example that could very much decide many  of these elections i think if you are a  vulnerable incumbent like catherine  cortez maska masto steve sisselak susie  lee dina titus you're looking at this  poll and biden's approval ratings and  you're wanting to take a step back from  the president and wanting to you know  focus on your own profile in the state  and your own record in order to really  distance yourself from him  i think that's a really good point a lot  of times you'll see a sitting president  come through a state on an election  swing a campaign swing do you think  that's likely this year  we'll have to see you know i thought it  was notable last week when president  biden traveled to cleveland to ohio and  i would say that ohio is not a swing  state in the same way nevada is ohio  definitely leans more to the right but  the democratic gubernatorial candidate  nan whaley and the democratic senate  candidate congressman tim ryan did not  appear with biden at the event he was  holding for union workers we know that  ohio is a big union state similar to  nevada but  tim ryan and nan whaley are two people  who are huge union advocates and they do  a lot within the union community in ohio  so to see other ohio democratic leaders  come and support biden at this union  event  but not see these two relatively  vulnerable  democratic candidates not appearing with  biden sort of shows you  you know where their head is at and  trying to distance themselves from him  now whaley and ryan and many other  candidates uh you know in similar  positions um whether it's biden or not  said that this was due to a scheduling  conflict so i think biden's going to  have to be very careful about who he  campaigns for and who he doesn't it  depends how he plays in the state  now about half this poll is being  released today and then they're coming  back tomorrow with the second half  first half covers the governor's race  the u.s senate race and these uh  these looks at biden's possibilities in  2024 as well as  uh approval rating for the supreme  court's performance is what sticks out  to you  in in that section of the poll  you know i think in that section of the  poll you have  how biden's running compared to how  incumbents are running notably catherine  cortez masto and steve sisilak he they  are running a few points ahead of him in  nevada whereas biden you know has a  slightly lower approval rating than them  and i think part of the reason for that  is that they have a bit of a distance  from washington obviously senator cortez  masto is in washington quite a bit  however she's still running as a  statewide figure same thing with  governor sisilak joe biden is seen as  the ultimate national democrat so it  makes sense for them to be maybe running  a few points ahead of him a number of  points ahead of him and i think you're  going to see democrats talking about  that when they're talking about how  biden's disapproval ratings or approval  ratings impact their candidates in fact  a lot of democrats working at senate  races have already pointed out to me  that their candidates that they're  working for are actually polling above  biden so i think it goes to show  how much of an albatross really the  former  excuse me the president is around a lot  of these candidates next but they're  going to be talking about how they're  pulling better than  he is i also think it's notable that  sisilak and cortez masto are polling  better than um you know their republican  challengers look i'm sure we will see  those numbers very much narrow as we get  closer to election day or or potentially  change however right now i think this  shows that they have an incumbent  advantage um in a state that's you know  one of the most competitive senate and  gubernatorial states going into november  and it shows how much the party  apparatus is really behind them we know  that the democratic machine is already  pouring money into nevada whether it's  at the senate level the governor's level  or the house level and i think in a way  that's being reflected in this poll  however don't expect these numbers to  remain this wide i think it's very  possible they could narrow as we get  closer to november  you know look democrats are  you know how much freedom unions get in  the state for example we know that the  culinary union is a giant in nevada and  there's a huge union presence especially  in las vegas so i think you could  potentially see  unions being affected you see democrats  talking about that  you see a lot of social issues also  being brought up such as the impact it  could have on the issue of abortion in  nevada we know that abortion is legal in  nevada but um democratic candidates  across the country even in states where  abortion is legal are still trying to  make this a campaign issue so i think  they'll continue to talk about that  and someone like sisilak would talk  about that um you know and i think in  terms of uh government spending how the  economy is run i think you could you  would likely see more of a conservative  figure in a joe lombardo um you know  maybe being a bit more fiscally  conservative when it comes to spending  in the state and in terms of what the  governor is focused on i mean that could  change as well we know that governor  sislek has talked about you know prime  and that issue however someone like joe  lombardo a sheriff is going to probably  have a more a deeper interest in that so  i think those are some of the potential  changes we could see  i want to thank you for your time today  i appreciate it very much  thank you

